A||||2||ejayerik||My 85 2M4||||5||
Z||000000||ejayerik||12-06-2001||07:42 PM||||Hey ppl. I bought an 85 2M4 recently for 175 bucks off ebay. You may have seen the red one for sale in Whittier Ca. Anyways, it was a real steal. The cluch, master cylinder, ignition module, ignition coils, wires, starter, fuel lines, fuel pump, distributer, and transmission have all been replaced as the vehicle's engine has 180,000 miles on it. It now has the Isuzu 5-speed trans in it. The problem I have is I need to know if there is going to be anything else that is prone to break in this year Fiero. I don't have much plans for it exept a new paint job and some headliner work. But the little things really bug me about this car. The temp guage goes up and down from almost redline to a quarter mark over and over again. But it only does this on the freeway. At idle, it sits right in the middle. It doesn't feel like it is overheating as the thermostat as also been replaced and there is good flow on the radiator. Could it be the sender unit or the temp. ring itself?<BR> Also, it is hard to get into gear. It doesn't grind but you have to "pop" it into gear. First is especially bad. I don't want to wear out this new clutch. Could this be a cracked shifter? It has this problem in all 5 gears including reverse. I heard if it is cracked, it has trouble shifting tward the passager side of the car. Side to side movement is not much hindered. Perhaps I need to lube the cable. Let me know so I can fix these problems before they get worse. I  would really like to say I truely got a good deal on this Fiero.<BR>              Thanks for your help guys,<BR>                                Erik||||reg||5
Z||000001||85 gt fiero||12-06-2001||08:49 PM||||Congratulations on your purchase, here are some of the things 85's break<P>1) Check your valve gasket for leaks cracks, they tend to wear out quickly<P>2) thermostats tend to stick a little you might need to lift the front end of the ground and pull the thermostat to "burp" it.<P>3) check your undercarrage for rust on the cradles, and or suspension componets<P>as to the shifting problem my old 84 had the same trouble too, new clutch in it and it still didnt shift easy, i think you might want to check your clutch fluid, and lubricate the the lines and see what happens.  Good Luck Randy, time to go to bed got school tommorw  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>RALLY CAR, FULL ROLL CAGE, SKID PLATES AND SOON TO BE BRIGHT YELLOW<p>[This message has been edited by 85 gt fiero (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||12-06-2001||09:14 PM||||On my '85 2.5 5-speed -- in less than two years -- I have fixed/replaced:<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>starter (twice) -- fried by proximity to the catalytic converter<BR><LI>timing cover seal (twice) -- pulley hub is scored<BR><LI>motor and transmission mounts -- old and tired; soaked with oil from leaking timing cover seal<BR><LI>valve cover gasket<BR><LI>engine torque strut (dogbone)<BR><LI>radiator and coolant tank<BR><LI>windshield washer pump<BR><LI>headliner<BR><LI>sail panels<BR><LI>right headlight motor (twice)<BR><LI>catalytic converter (my fault)<BR><LI>door window "dew wipe" strips<BR><LI>dash speakers<BR><LI>radio<BR><LI>brake pads and right rear rotor<BR><LI>headlight relay<BR><LI>cylinder head (blown gasket, cracked head)<BR><LI>clutch master and slave cylinders (beware <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015204.html" TARGET=_blank>short pushrod syndrome</A>)<BR></UL><P><B>But I still love it!</B><P>I think this is a fairly representative list -- typical problems on a 16-year-old car.<P><BR><I>Edit</I> Oh, yeah -- with high mileage, your timing gears could go at any moment if they haven't already been replaced.<P><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
